      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "A previous survey had indicated high rates of low back pain (LBP) in Iranian nurses and office workers."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To explore possible risk factors, we carried out a longitudinal study of the same subjects."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Baseline information about risk factors and recent history of LBP was collected by self-administered questionnaire from 246 nurses and 182 office workers. Approximately 12 months later, 385 (90%) answered a second questionnaire about LBP in the past month. Predictors of LBP at follow-up were assessed by Poisson regression and summarised by prevalence rate ratios (PRRs) with 95% confidence intervals (CIs)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "In a regression model that included all risk factors, the strongest predictor of LBP at follow-up was report of recent LBP at baseline. In addition, LBP was associated with older age, adverse beliefs about the work-relatedness of LBP (PRR 1.3, 95%CI 1.0-1.5), and incentives from piecework or bonuses (PRR 1.4, 95%CI 1.1-1.6). When baseline report of LBP was omitted from the model, associations were also observed with tendency to somatise, poor mental health and time pressures at work."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Our findings support the importance of psychosocial risk factors for LBP, including adverse health beliefs and working conditions that encourage higher output."
        }
      ]},
